shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | `status: OK` measures attempted jobs only; cross-check `okCount` for SSD capture, and the expected count is not a constant — it moved 28→76 the same day | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"ZoteroInjestion Promoted to a Real Submodule, Pinned to a Snapshot Branch\"\naliases: [LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion submodule, mac-snapshot-2026-09-09 branch, submodule tracking main vs snapshot, referenced in 4 files, erash11/.claude junk repo deleted]\ntags: [git, submodule, repo-hygiene, macos, workflow, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# ZoteroInjestion Promoted to a Real Submodule, Pinned to a Snapshot Branch\n\nDuring the 2026-09-11 `~/DEV` cleanup, `LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion` — referenced from four files and previously in an ambiguous nested state — was converted into a proper git submodule. The conversion left one decision open: the submodule points at branch `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`, and whether it should track `main` instead was deferred to the user. Separately, a junk `erash11/.claude` GitHub repository was deleted.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Four inbound references made the ambiguity load-bearing.** A directory referenced from four files but not declared as either a tracked subdirectory or a submodule is a boundary the tooling has to infer, and inference is what produces the nested-repo hazards seen elsewhere in this stack.\n- **The conversion replaced inference with a declaration** — a `.gitmodules` entry stating explicitly what the parent repo's relationship to that directory is.\n- **The pinned branch is a snapshot, not a line of development.** `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` is a point-in-time capture from the cross-machine merge; a submodule tracking it will never receive upstream work landed on `main`.\n- **The branch choice was deferred rather than guessed.** Pinning to a snapshot is defensible (reproducible, frozen) and tracking `main` is defensible (receives fixes); the difference is a project-ownership question the session correctly declined to settle.\n- **A junk `erash11/.claude` GitHub repo was deleted** in the same hygiene pass, removing a remote that served no purpose and could be mistaken for the real `~/.claude` backup target.\n\n## Details\n\nThe submodule conversion resolves the same class of problem as the explicit `.gitignore` entry for `.claude/worktrees/` and the stray home-directory `.git`: a repository boundary that the tooling was inferring from filesystem layout rather than reading from a declaration. Inferred boundaries fail in both directions — a nested repository can be accidentally staged into its parent, or a genuinely separate project can be silently swallowed as ordinary tracked files — and in both cases the symptom appears far from the cause, typically as an inexplicable file count or a checkout that materialises the wrong thing. Declaring the relationship in `.gitmodules` means every subsequent clone, status, and checkout consults a stated fact rather than reconstructing an intent.\n\nThe open branch question is the more interesting residue, because pinning to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` encodes a decision that will quietly expire. A snapshot branch is created to capture a state during a migration or merge; it is by construction not maintained. A submodule pointed at it is reproducible today and increasingly stale thereafter, and — critically — nothing will announce the drift, because a submodule pinned to a branch that receives no commits looks identical to a submodule that is perfectly up to date. This is the same shape as an install-time capture of a host fact: correct at the moment it is taken, silently wrong once the host moves, and diagnosable only by inspecting the pin rather than by observing a failure. If the snapshot pin is kept deliberately, the reason belongs written down next to it, in the manner of the `DexaProject` manifest exception; if it is kept by default, it will outlive the memory of why it was chosen.\n\nThe `erash11/.claude` deletion is minor in itself but consistent with the day's direction. A stray remote that duplicates the name of a real, actively-pushed backup target is a naming collision waiting to misdirect a future operator or script, and removing it is cheaper than documenting it. It sits alongside the same-day removal of stale manifest entries for `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` two days earlier: repo and manifest hygiene runs in both directions, and retiring dead references is the half more often skipped.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[connections/git-repository-boundaries-should-be-explicit]] — The general rule this conversion instantiates: replace an inferred boundary with a declared one.\n- [[concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene]] — The nested-repo hazard that an undeclared repository inside a repository produces, and the explicit-path staging response.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The `~/DEV` repo topology this submodule now sits inside.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The companion GitHub-side hygiene pass; `erash11/.claude` is another repo removed from that account.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The same both-directions hygiene principle applied to manifest entries, including retiring stale ones.\n- [[connections/install-time-environment-capture-rots-silently]] — Why a pin to a frozen snapshot decays without ever reporting an error.\n- [[connections/the-default-is-the-real-decision]] — A branch choice left as a pin expires with human memory unless the reason is recorded.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-11.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:10): `LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion` made a real git submodule after being found referenced in 4 files; the submodule points at branch `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`, with the choice left for the user to decide; junk `erash11/.claude` GitHub repo deleted.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\"\naliases: [CRASHBOX, SSD backup baseline, AssistantBackup, backup manifest path, volume rename mid-backup, rsync incremental resume, diskutil rename, local-only backup, Drive upload deferral]\ntags: [backup, macos, storage, automation, rsync, workflow, machine-restore]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\n\nOn 2026-08-11, as part of a machine-restore checklist closeout, an external SSD was renamed to `CRASHBOX` and used to capture the first full local backup baseline at `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`. The rename occurred while a backup run was already in flight; the run was restarted against the new path and completed clean (OK, 0 errors) at 21:25. The session also established which parts of the backup pipeline require network connectivity and which do not.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **First full CRASHBOX baseline completed clean:** OK with 0 errors at 21:25, after the backup manifest was repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup` and the in-flight run restarted.\n- **Mid-backup volume rename is safe.** Already-copied data survives the rename, and rsync's incremental behavior means a restarted run — or one interrupted by power loss — resumes rather than redoing completed work.\n- **Volume names containing spaces work fine in the PowerShell backup manifest;** the rename to `CRASHBOX` was cosmetic, not a correctness fix.\n- **Network dependency is confined to one step.** The SSD copy is fully local; Google Drive writes cache locally and upload later; only the git commit/push step needs the network, and it self-heals on the next nightly run. The machine could therefore be safely disconnected and shut down mid-pipeline.\n- **The rename was performed via a one-off manual-mode approval** rather than a standing `Bash(diskutil rename:*)` allow rule, since no recurrence was expected.\n\n## Details\n\nThe backup pipeline has three write targets with different durability and connectivity characteristics, and the session made this stratification explicit. The local SSD copy is synchronous and offline-capable. The Google Drive leg writes to a locally-cached CloudStorage path and defers the actual upload, so it neither blocks nor requires connectivity at run time — the same local-cache-then-upload behavior that previously caused a receipt read-back race when a job read a freshly-written receipt before Drive had flushed it. Only the git commit and push step genuinely needs the network, and because the backup job runs nightly, a failed push is self-healing: the next run retries it. This meant the correct answer to \"do I need internet before shutting down?\" was no, with a single deferred and self-recovering exception.\n\nThe mid-run rename was the session's operationally interesting move. Renaming a mounted volume changes the `/Volumes/<name>` mount path, which invalidates any manifest entry or in-progress run referencing the old path — but it does not touch the copied files themselves. Because the backup uses rsync semantics, restarting against the corrected path performs an incremental pass over the already-transferred data rather than a full recopy, so the cost of the restart is proportional to what remained rather than to the total. This same property makes the run resilient to power interruption, which matters for a first full baseline that transfers the largest volume of data it ever will. The rename itself was cosmetic; the manifest tolerated the prior name, spaces included.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The same backup subsystem, where a Google Drive CloudStorage write-lag raced a receipt read-back and the job was moved under `launchd`; this article records the local-SSD leg and its offline-capable characteristics.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The rename was gated by the permission system and resolved via a one-off manual-mode approval rather than a standing allow rule.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The broader scheduled-automation stack (nightly backup task, reindex, cloud checks) that this baseline run belongs to.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why durable, long-running backup work belongs in an OS-scheduled job rather than an in-session background command.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): SSD renamed to `CRASHBOX`, manifest repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`, in-flight run restarted, network-dependency analysis (local SSD / deferred Drive upload / self-healing git push), first full baseline completed OK with 0 errors at 21:25.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Vendored Skill Hand-Install Pattern (~/.agents/skills → Claude + Codex)\"\naliases: [hand-install skills, davidondrej skills repo, npx skills add blocked, skill symlink shape, .skill-lock.json missing hand-installed, persistent-localhost, anti-sleep, 58 skills vetted, 151 to 156 skills]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, installation, macos, symlinks, tooling, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Vendored Skill Hand-Install Pattern (~/.agents/skills → Claude + Codex)\n\nOn 2026-09-04, the `davidondrej/skills` GitHub repository (58 skills) was vetted against the existing environment and five skills were installed by hand after the official `npx skills add` installer was blocked by the auto-mode permission classifier. The session established a working install shape — vendor directory plus two layers of symlink — verified it empirically rather than by assumption, and documented a real cost of the manual route: hand-installed skills are invisible to the upstream lockfile and therefore to `skills update`.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **53 of 58 skills rejected, with reasons by category:** duplicates of already-installed capability (superpowers, session-handoff, CreateSkill, codex plugin, launchd), dependence on tools absent from this machine (bb, cmux, Herdr, Pi, Cursor, Postgres), a paid DeepAPI key requirement (`risky-changes` and all ten `research-and-web` skills), and hardcoding of the author's own name (`file-tree`, `stop-overthinking`, `short`, `remind`).\n- **Five installed, taking the count 151 → 156:** `persistent-localhost` (runs dev servers as launchd jobs, replacing `&`/`nohup`), `anti-sleep` (`caffeinate` for long or overnight runs), and three slash-only skills — `/decisions`, `/before-building`, `/next-decision`.\n- **`global-agent-guardrails` was skipped for a concrete conflict,** not redundancy alone: `dcg` already covers the ground, and the skill's denylist blocks `security find-generic-password`, which the Fireflies key read depends on.\n- **The working install shape:** copy `SKILL.md` plus `scripts/` and `agents/` into `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`, `chmod +x` the scripts, symlink *relatively* into `~/.claude/skills/<name>`, then symlink `~/.codex/skills/<name>` at `~/.claude/skills/<name>` — every Codex entry points at the Claude path, not at `~/.agents/skills` directly.\n- **Hand-installed skills are absent from `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`,** so `skills update` will not see them. Registering them retroactively is possible by running the official installer behind a `!` prefix to bypass the classifier; it may simply report \"already installed.\"\n- **Both installs were tested, not assumed:** `persistent-localhost` served a real HTTP 200 on port 5199 and stopped cleanly; `anti-sleep` reached `ASSERTIONS=active` and stopped leaving no stray plists.\n\n## Details\n\nThe vetting ratio — 5 accepted out of 58 — is the substantive finding, and the four rejection categories are reusable as a triage rubric for any third-party skill bundle. Duplication is the largest bucket and the cheapest to check, since an existing capability makes the new skill pure listing overhead against the description budget documented in [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]]. Missing-dependency and paid-key rejections are hard constraints discoverable by reading the skill body. The author-name-hardcoding category is the least obvious and the most instructive: a skill written for personal use may embed its author's identity in prompts or paths, which makes it silently wrong rather than broken when installed elsewhere. The `global-agent-guardrails` rejection is a fifth, sharper category — active interference, where the skill's denylist would have broken an unrelated working integration (the Fireflies keychain read). That is a case where \"we already have coverage\" understates the reason to decline.\n\nThe symlink topology is worth stating precisely because it is not the obvious arrangement. The canonical files live once in `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`; `~/.claude/skills/<name>` is a *relative* symlink into that directory; and `~/.codex/skills/<name>` symlinks to the Claude path rather than to the vendor path. Routing Codex through Claude rather than pointing both harnesses at the vendor directory means the Claude path is the single indirection point — moving or replacing the underlying skill requires updating one link, not two. The insistence on a relative link for the Claude leg matters given this environment's history with committed symlinks: absolute links that encode a home directory are exactly what broke during the Windows→Mac migration described in [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]].\n\nThe lockfile gap is the genuine cost of hand-installing and should be recorded wherever the install is documented. `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json` is the manifest `skills update` consults, so a skill installed outside the installer is functionally present but permanently unmanaged — it will never receive an upstream fix. This is the same silent-omission shape as a backup manifest that does not list a folder: every managed item reports success, and the unmanaged item is invisible precisely because nothing knows to look for it. The offered remedy (re-running the official installer behind `!` purely to register the entries) resolves the bookkeeping without repeating the file operations. A final procedural note from the same session: two prior claims recorded in `project_vendored-skill-install.md` were disproven during this work and corrected in Tier A memory, which is the correct disposition for memory that testing contradicts.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The `rm -rf` guard rule that forced the install script into a copy-only shape, and the `>`-in-Python false positive hit during the same session.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The auto-mode classifier that blocked `npx skills add`, and the `!` one-off bypass offered as the lockfile-registration route.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — Why installing marginal skills is not free: every listed skill consumes the session description budget, making the 53 rejections a direct saving.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — Prior symlink trouble in this same skills tree; motivates the relative-link discipline used here.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-meta-skills-bundle]] — The earlier precedent of cherry-picking a subset from a multi-skill bundle rather than accepting an all-or-nothing install.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — Tier A is where the two disproven install claims were corrected.\n- [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] — The lockfile gap is an instance: every installed skill works, yet the update path cannot see them.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Skill review and install session (13:18): all 58 `davidondrej/skills` vetted with four rejection categories, five installed (151 → 156), `global-agent-guardrails` skipped for its `security find-generic-password` denylist conflict, `npx skills add` blocked by the auto-mode classifier, the `~/.agents/skills` → `~/.claude/skills` → `~/.codex/skills` symlink shape, the `dcg` `rm -rf` rule forcing a copy-only script, `.skill-lock.json` invisibility with the `!`-prefixed registration remedy, empirical tests of `persistent-localhost` (HTTP 200 on port 5199) and `anti-sleep` (`ASSERTIONS=active`), and two disproven claims corrected in `project_vendored-skill-install.md`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Tarball Backups and User Review Before Destructive Git Operations\"\naliases: [pre-reset tarball, agents-skills-before-reset.tar.gz, doctor-backup directory, stop for user eyes, destructive git op checkpoint, blast radius misprediction, staged backup naming]\ntags: [git, backup, workflow, safety, recovery, claude-code, process]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-08.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-08\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# Tarball Backups and User Review Before Destructive Git Operations\n\nThe 2026-08-08 `~/.agents/skills` incident demonstrated the value of two safety practices used together: taking a filesystem tarball snapshot before each destructive step, and stopping for explicit user authorization before running irreversible git commands. When `git reset --hard origin/main` corrupted all 21 mattpocock skills, recovery was immediate because a pre-reset tarball existed; and the pause for review was retroactively justified because the agent had mispredicted the reset's blast radius.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **One tarball per destructive step, named for the step it precedes:** `agents-skills-before-codex-fix.tar.gz` (pre-`sed`) and `agents-skills-before-reset.tar.gz` (pre-`reset --hard`), both preserved under `~/.claude/doctor-backup-20260807-135924/`. Naming the snapshot after the operation it guards makes the correct restore point unambiguous during recovery.\n- **Filesystem tarball, not a git stash or branch:** because the failure mode was git itself materializing tracked symlinks as text files, any git-native checkpoint would have been subject to the same corruption. A tarball captures the working tree as the OS sees it, outside git's file-type semantics.\n- **Explicit user authorization was sought twice** (\"You're okay to make the change\", repeated after a re-explanation) before the destructive change proceeded — a deliberate checkpoint rather than autonomous execution.\n- **The pause was vindicated by a real misprediction:** the agent predicted 76 local-only files would survive `reset --hard`; 73 did not, because `origin/main` tracked those paths as symlinks rather than leaving them untracked. Predicted blast radius diverged from actual.\n- **Recovery was complete and verifiable:** restore returned HEAD to `459c9ba` (= `origin/main`) with all 21 symlinks resolving and 133 `SKILL.md` files reachable — verification by counting reachable artifacts, not by assuming the untar succeeded.\n\n## Details\n\nThe general principle is that the confidence with which an agent can predict a destructive command's effects is not a reliable input to whether a backup is needed. In this case the reasoning about `reset --hard` was specific, plausible, and wrong in a way that only surfaced after execution: files that looked local-only from the working tree were in fact tracked upstream as symlinks, so `--hard` overwrote them. A prediction stated with confidence about `git reset`, `git clean`, `rm -rf`, or a mass `sed` should therefore be treated as a hypothesis, and the backup taken regardless. The cost of the tarball is seconds; the cost of its absence in this incident would have been the loss of 21 skills and 933 files with no clean path back.\n\nThe choice of a filesystem tarball over a git-native checkpoint is not incidental. The corruption mechanism was git's own working-tree materialization behavior under `core.symlinks=false` — symlinks written out as plain text files. A `git stash` or a temporary branch stores content through the same index and object machinery that produced the corruption, so restoring from one would reproduce the text-file forms rather than the symlinks. A tarball, by contrast, records what the operating system reports: real symlinks as symlinks. The general rule is that a backup should live at a layer beneath the one that is suspected of misbehaving.\n\nVerification after restore followed the same discipline. Rather than treating a successful `tar -x` exit code as proof of recovery, the check enumerated the properties that define a working state: HEAD at the expected commit (`459c9ba`), all 21 symlinks resolving to real directories, and 133 `SKILL.md` files reachable through those links. This makes the recovery claim falsifiable — the same standard applied to the original diagnosis, where a claimed 29-file scope turned out to be 235 occurrences across 73 files.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — The corruption these backups recovered from; the tarball is what made the landmine survivable rather than catastrophic.\n- [[concepts/codex-path-rewrite-incident]] — The pre-`sed` tarball guarded the first destructive step of the same session, before the reset was even contemplated.\n- [[connections/check-version-control-before-mass-editing]] — The complementary pre-flight discipline: consult the remote before editing, snapshot the tree before destroying.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — Verifying recovery by counting resolvable symlinks and reachable `SKILL.md` files rather than trusting the extraction's exit code.\n- [[concepts/accidental-git-init-home-directory]] — Another case where inspecting actual state before a destructive filesystem action (confirming the `.git` folder was empty) was the deciding safety check.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-08.md]] — `.Codex` fix and reset incident (11:33): two staged tarballs (`agents-skills-before-codex-fix.tar.gz`, `agents-skills-before-reset.tar.gz`) preserved in `~/.claude/doctor-backup-20260807-135924/`; user authorization sought twice before the destructive change; `reset --hard` blast-radius misprediction (76 predicted survivors vs. 73 lost); restore verified at HEAD `459c9ba` with 21 symlinks resolving and 133 `SKILL.md` reachable.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/subagent-driven-development.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Subagent-Driven Development (Two-Stage Review)\"\naliases: [Subagent-Driven Development skill, spec compliance reviewer, code quality reviewer, two-stage review process]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, development-methodology, code-review, subagents]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-24.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-24\nupdated: 2026-04-24\n---\n\n# Subagent-Driven Development (Two-Stage Review)\n\nSubagent-Driven Development is a Claude Code skill/methodology for implementing tasks from a written plan via subagents, followed by two independent review passes - a spec-compliance review and a separate code-quality review - before a task is considered done. It was used on 2026-04-24 to complete the final task of the [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]].\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Tasks are drawn from a written plan document (e.g., `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-04-24-perch-weight-room-domain.md`), with each task implemented by a subagent.\n- After implementation, a spec-compliance reviewer checks the result against the plan's stated requirements using a checklist; Task 9 passed this stage 12/12.\n- A second, separately-focused code-quality reviewer then reviews the same implementation for correctness and quality issues independent of the spec checklist.\n- On Task 9, the code-quality reviewer caught a real bug - two chart-cleanup arrays in `report.html.j2` missing a `\"trend-wr\"` entry - that had passed both the implementer's self-review and the spec-compliance review.\n- After the implementer fixed the flagged bug, the code-quality reviewer re-reviewed and passed it, completing the task.\n\n## Details\n\nThe methodology separates \"did this match the plan?\" from \"is this code actually correct and well-written?\" into two distinct review passes, each performed independently. This separation matters because a fully spec-compliant implementation can still contain bugs that the spec's acceptance checklist simply doesn't enumerate - in this case, the plan's requirements for Task 9 didn't explicitly call out every chart-cleanup array that needed a `\"trend-wr\"` entry, so the spec-compliance reviewer had nothing to flag even though it checked all 12 of its listed items. The code-quality reviewer, working from a different lens (general code correctness rather than a fixed checklist), caught the omission.\n\nThis two-stage structure is a development-process instance of a broader pattern also seen in data-pipeline work: a single review/verification pass, however thorough by its own criteria, can miss errors that a differently-focused second pass would catch. See [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] for how this Task 9 experience parallels an earlier citation-verification fix in [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]. Practically, the methodology produced a clean outcome for the [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]]: with Task 9's bug fixed and re-reviewed, the full 9-task feature reached 26/26 passing tests across 11 commits.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]] - The project whose final task (Task 9) was completed using this methodology.\n- [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] - Generalizes this two-stage review pattern alongside an earlier, differently-shaped verification lesson.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-24.md]] - Task 9 implementation and two-stage review session (17:06).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Environment Maintenance\"\naliases: [doctor diagnostics, plugin cache migration, mcp re-auth, effort setting, github MCP plugin, GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN, gh CLI vs MCP, fable-5 model switch, advisor incompatibility, orphan temp_github folders, github MCP 401 header, OAuth fallback disabled]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, maintenance, infrastructure]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-03.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-07-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Claude Code Environment Maintenance\n\nPeriodic maintenance of the Claude Code environment surfaces configuration-drift issues that accumulate silently between active sessions. Four notable maintenance passes have been documented: a 2026-06-10 session that resolved plugin cache migration, stale MCP authentications, and temp-directory accumulation; a 2026-06-03 session that diagnosed and resolved a missing `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` for the GitHub MCP plugin; a 2026-06-09 session that switched the default model to Fable 5 and discovered that the advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with Fable 5 requests; and a 2026-07-09 observation of the GitHub MCP plugin failing to reconnect with an HTTP 401 because a configured `Authorization` header disables OAuth fallback. Most issues are caught via `/doctor` and resolved through `/mcp`, `/plugins`, or targeted configuration changes.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/doctor` flagged 4 issues on 2026-06-10: three claude.ai MCP servers (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) needing re-authentication, and the `cli-printing-press` plugin not found at its expected versioned cache path.\n- Claude Code migrated its plugin cache from a flat layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\`) to a versioned layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\<plugin>\\<version>\\`), likely due to a Claude Code update. Plugins installed under the old layout fail version checks even though their skills may still work via a fallback path; the fix is to reinstall via `/plugins`.\n- Four stale `temp_github_*` directories were found in the plugins cache, left over from interrupted installs in late May/early June — safe to delete once confirmed unused. Interrupted plugin downloads can both lose a plugin's cache entry and leave orphan `temp_github_*` folders; `/plugin` reinstall + `/reload-plugins` restores the cache, and the orphan folders require manual deletion.\n- For MCP servers not actively used from Claude Code (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar), disconnecting them in claude.ai's connector settings permanently silences `/doctor` re-auth warnings and eliminates the tool-schema token overhead of loading unused MCP tool definitions each session.\n- The `compound-engineering` plugin was installed via `/plugin` and activated with `/reload-plugins`, adding `ce-*` skills/agents for planning, code review, commits, and PR workflows. `/effort` was set to \"high\" as the new session default.\n- **GitHub MCP plugin vs. `gh` CLI (2026-06-03):** the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP plugin requires a `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable to pass `/doctor`. If all GitHub operations use the `gh` CLI directly (authenticated via `gh auth login`; scopes: gist, read:org, repo, workflow), the MCP plugin is redundant — the cleanest fix is disabling it with `\"github@claude-plugins-official\": false` in `.claude/settings.json`. Alternatively, set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` from an existing PAT (e.g., reuse `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` if it has matching scopes).\n- **Fable 5 + medium effort + advisor incompatibility (2026-06-09):** default model switched to Fable 5, effort set to medium. The advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with the Fable 5 request model — requests return 400 errors — and was disabled. When switching request models, the advisor model must also be verified for compatibility or disabled; mismatched advisor+request combinations produce API errors that are not immediately obvious as a configuration conflict.\n- **GitHub MCP 401 with Authorization header disables OAuth (2026-07-09):** the `plugin:github:github` MCP server failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 — the server rejected the configured `Authorization` header, and OAuth fallback is disabled whenever `headers.Authorization` is explicitly set. Fix: check/refresh the token, or remove the `headers.Authorization` config entirely to re-enable OAuth negotiation.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `/doctor` command acts as a quick health check across MCP connections, plugin installs, and cache state. On 2026-06-10 it caught a cache-format mismatch that was not the user's fault: a Claude Code update changed how plugins are cached on disk (from a flat directory per plugin to a versioned subdirectory), and `cli-printing-press` had been installed before that change. The plugin's files were technically present, but the version-check logic looked in the new versioned path and didn't find them — the recommended fix was simply to reinstall the plugin via `/plugins` so it lands in the new layout. The MCP re-auth findings (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) were treated as low priority: rather than re-authenticating connectors that may not be exercised from Claude Code at all, the suggested path was to disconnect them at the source (claude.ai connector settings), which both stops the recurring `/doctor` warning and reduces the standing tool-schema footprint in every session.\n\nOn 2026-06-03, a separate `/doctor` run flagged the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP server as invalid — specifically, no `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable was set. Investigation confirmed that all GitHub interactions in this environment already flow through the `gh` CLI (authenticated separately in `~/.config/gh/`), making the GitHub MCP plugin effectively unused. The two resolution options are: (1) disable the plugin entirely in `.claude/settings.json` — the simplest fix when the plugin genuinely adds no value; or (2) set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` using an existing PAT with compatible scopes, avoiding a new token generation. In this case, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` already existed with sufficient scopes, so `setx GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN <value>` was used. Note: `setx` writes to the Windows registry immediately but requires fully restarting VS Code for the change to propagate to the Claude Code process — see [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] for the stale-environment gotcha this can trigger.\n\nOn 2026-06-09, a maintenance session switched the default model from Sonnet-tier to Fable 5 and changed effort to medium. The advisor feature, which provides secondary-model review of responses, uses a separately-specified model (opus-4-8 in this configuration). When the request model is Fable 5, the opus-4-8 advisor returns 400 API errors because the two models are incompatible in the advisor/request pairing. The advisor was disabled as a result. The lesson: model switches require verifying the full model configuration stack — not just the primary request model but also any secondary models (advisor, sub-agent defaults, tool-use overrides) that may have incompatible pairings.\n\nOn 2026-07-09, the GitHub MCP plugin surfaced a distinct failure mode from the 2026-06-03 missing-token case: here a token/header *was* configured, but the plugin failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 because the server rejected the supplied `Authorization` header. The non-obvious mechanism is that setting `headers.Authorization` explicitly disables the plugin's OAuth fallback — the plugin assumes a manually-supplied header means the user wants header-based auth exclusively, so it does not fall back to OAuth negotiation when the header is rejected. The result is a hard 401 with no automatic recovery. The two fixes are to refresh the token behind the header, or to remove the `headers.Authorization` config so OAuth negotiation is re-enabled. This reinforces the broader 2026-06-03 finding that the GitHub MCP plugin is often redundant when `gh` CLI already handles all GitHub operations — disabling the plugin outright sidesteps both the missing-token and the header-401 failure modes.\n\nTogether these maintenance passes confirm the same operational pattern: the `/doctor` command's value is proportional to how regularly it is run and how promptly its findings are triaged. Issues like the plugin cache migration, the unused GitHub MCP plugin, the advisor incompatibility, the header-401 reconnect failure, and the orphan temp folders can accumulate silently for weeks without causing visible failures, but each unresolved warning represents either a real reliability risk or unnecessary token overhead that degrades session quality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Skill creation later on the same 2026-06-10 maintenance day depended on plugins and skills registering correctly, which this maintenance pass helped ensure.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — The on-ramp automation pilot run later on 2026-06-10 operates inside this same Claude Code harness.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) setup of the persistent-memory hook system that lives in the same `~/.claude/` configuration this maintenance pass audited.\n- [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] — The Windows registry env-var propagation gotcha encountered while applying the `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` fix on 2026-06-03.\n- [[concepts/book-advisor-system]] — The `/advisor` invocation on 2026-07-09 that surfaced the GitHub MCP 401 also confirmed the current advisor library state (one persona).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — `/doctor` diagnostics, plugin cache fix, MCP re-auth guidance, `compound-engineering` plugin install, `/effort` default change.\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the earlier memory-compiler hook setup referenced above.\n- [[daily/2026-06-03.md]] — GitHub MCP plugin `/doctor` warning, `gh` CLI vs. MCP investigation, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` reuse via `setx`, and stale VS Code environment diagnosis (~15:00 session).\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — Fable 5 model switch (14:07): effort set to medium, opus-4-8 advisor disabled due to 400 API incompatibility with Fable 5, orphan `temp_github_*` folders confirmed and deleted.\n- [[daily/2026-07-09.md]] — GitHub MCP 401 reconnect failure (09:35): server rejects configured `Authorization` header, OAuth fallback disabled when `headers.Authorization` is set; fix is token refresh or header removal.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"status: OK Is Not Proof of a Full Backup — Read okCount\"\naliases: [okCount cross-check, status OK insufficient, backup job count baseline drift, error count cannot detect missing SSD, 9 vs 31 vs 76 jobs, two independent completeness axes]\ntags: [backup, automation, verification, macos, monitoring, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# status: OK Is Not Proof of a Full Backup — Read okCount\n\nThe KeystoneOS daily backup reports a `status` field and an `okCount`, and the 2026-09-09 session restated as explicit policy that the former is insufficient on its own. A run can report `status: OK` with zero errors while having captured only a fraction of the intended data, because the error count measures the outcome of attempted jobs and cannot detect a job that was never attempted. The cross-check is `okCount`: roughly 31 jobs meant the CRASHBOX SSD was captured, roughly 9–10 meant it was absent and only repo pushes plus Google Drive snapshots ran.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error count is structurally blind to a missing target.** An unmounted SSD removes whole job classes from the run; every remaining job succeeds, so the aggregate verdict is clean and wrong.\n- **`okCount` is the completeness signal, `status` is the execution signal.** They answer different questions and both must be read.\n- **The expected job count is not a constant.** The same session that codified the tell moved the full-run baseline from 28 to 76 jobs by adding 49 manifest entries, so any remembered number expires.\n- **There are two independent completeness axes:** whether the SSD was mounted (job classes present or absent) and whether the manifest is complete (folders in scope or not). A run can be full on one axis and empty on the other.\n\n## Details\n\nThis lesson has now been learned twice from different directions, and the pairing is what makes it durable. The 2026-09-04 finding recorded in [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] arrived at the job count from the hardware side: a charge-only USB-C cable left the CRASHBOX SSD unmounted, the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes silently did not exist, and a clean run saved nothing that was uncommitted, non-repo, or gitignored. The 2026-09-09 finding arrives from the manifest side: the SSD was fine, but 49 folders were never in the instruction set, so their absence was equally invisible to the error count. In both cases the failure is that the denominator shrank rather than the numerator falling, which no ratio-free success indicator can express — the same defect described generally in [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]].\n\nThe baseline drift is the practical complication. On 2026-09-07 the tell was hardened by writing the *mechanism* into `SKILL.md` — the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` classes exist only when the drive is mounted — precisely so the check would not depend on remembering that 31 is good. Two days later the manifest expansion vindicated that decision by moving a full run to 76 jobs, which would have invalidated a memorized threshold overnight. The generalizable rule, stated in [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]], is that a quantity used as a health check should be recorded at whatever abstraction level is stable: \"the SSD job classes are missing\" survives a manifest change, \"expect 31\" does not. The correct reading of a backup run is therefore three-part — did the attempted jobs succeed (`status`), were the SSD-dependent job classes present (`okCount` shape), and does the manifest still cover every folder that matters (`manifest-unmanaged` entries) — with no single field standing in for the other two.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The origin of the job-count tell and its `CAPTURE`/`STATE-SSD` mechanism, discovered from the unmounted-drive side.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The manifest-side instance from the same session, which also reset the expected job count from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — A sibling case where a different backup status field answered a narrower question than the operator's.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern: an accurate field reporting on a narrower predicate than the one being asked.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — Why a clean verdict over a shrunken work set is the recurring shape here.\n- [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]] — Why the tell was written as a mechanism rather than a threshold, validated by the baseline change.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): explicit lesson that `status: OK` alone is not proof of a full backup and `okCount` must be cross-checked (~31 jobs = SSD captured, ~9–10 = SSD absent with only repo pushes and Drive snapshots), that the error count cannot detect a missing SSD, and that manifest coverage is a parallel silent failure mode; job count moved 28 → 76 in the same session.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/skills-repo-worktree-branch-cleanup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Skills Repo Worktree and Branch Cleanup (dcg branch-force-delete Gate)\"\naliases: [dcg core.git branch-force-delete, agent cannot delete branches, worktree removal vs branch delete, 8 merged worktrees removed, stale worktree-* branches, Codex stop-gate review loop cancelled, Telos Update guard, LIFEOS TOOLS missing entries, execution.jsonl audit]\ntags: [git, claude-code, skills, worktree, hooks, permissions, cleanup]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-15\nupdated: 2026-08-15\n---\n\n# Skills Repo Worktree and Branch Cleanup (dcg branch-force-delete Gate)\n\nThe closing 2026-08-15 session (20:32) wrapped up a Codex-driven cleanup of `~/.claude/skills` following browser-routing, LifeOS-restore, and Telos-guard work. All eight merged worktrees were removed, leaving only `main`, and all work through commit `8b31d4f` was confirmed merged and pushed with a clean working tree matching `origin/main`. The session's durable finding is an asymmetry in the permission layer: worktree *removal* is agent-permitted, but branch *deletion* is blocked by the `dcg` hook rule `core.git:branch-force-delete`, so the eight stale `worktree-*` branches must be deleted by the user directly.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`core.git:branch-force-delete` blocks agent-initiated branch deletion.** The `dcg` hook rule gates the delete; worktree removal is unaffected. The result is a partial cleanup that an agent cannot finish on its own — it can remove the directories but not the refs.\n- **Eight stale branches left for manual deletion:** `worktree-decouple-inference`, `worktree-fix-telos-guard`, `worktree-reconcile-lifeos-docs`, `worktree-retire-lifeos-skills`, `worktree-telos-guard-partial`, `worktree-telos-precondition-writability`, `worktree-telos-update-preconditions`, `worktree-triage-sync-detection` — run as `git -C ~/.claude/skills branch -d <names>`.\n- **The Codex stop-gate review loop was cancelled** (`task-msupzx5t-beim0g`) rather than run for another round — a deliberate stop on a review loop with diminishing returns, paired with an explicit decision to leave a confirmed-but-minor finding unfixed.\n- **Known-and-deferred defect:** `Telos/Workflows/Update.md` guards with `[ -w ]` but line 127 *reads* the file, so a write-only file passes the precondition and then fails at the changelog view. The fix is an added `[ -r \"$T/updates.md\" ] || abort ...`. The Telos Update sequence is also three non-atomic steps, documented in-file with recovery steps and deliberately left as-is.\n- **Two unaudited surfaces carried forward:** 45 skills append to `LIFEOS/MEMORY/SKILLS/execution.jsonl` and have never been audited; five `LIFEOS/TOOLS` entries predating this work are missing (`notioncli`, `fabric`, `ghcli`, `md2html`, `data-cli`) and need restoring or removing.\n\n## Details\n\nThe permission asymmetry is the reusable lesson. A cleanup that reads as a single operation — \"remove the merged worktrees\" — is in fact two operations against different git objects, and only one of them is agent-permitted. Removing a worktree deletes a directory and a registration entry; deleting a branch destroys a ref that is the only remaining pointer to a commit chain, which is precisely the destructive class the `dcg` guard exists to gate. An agent that treats the two as one step will report a completed cleanup while leaving eight dangling branches behind. The correct handling is what happened here: perform the permitted half, then hand the user a copy-pasteable command for the gated half rather than attempting a workaround. This is the same narrowest-sufficient-escalation reasoning documented in [[connections/narrowest-sufficient-escalation-over-blanket-bypass]] — the block is respected and routed to the human rather than bypassed, because the operation is genuinely destructive and infrequent enough not to warrant a standing permission.\n\nThe `[ -w ]` versus `[ -r ]` guard defect in `Telos/Workflows/Update.md` is a precondition-check bug of the same family as the swallowed-stderr `curl` check documented in [[concepts/browser-automation-stack-audit-mac]]: a check that runs, passes, and does not actually verify the condition the downstream code depends on. Writability and readability are distinct POSIX permissions, and a workflow that writes at one point and reads at another needs both asserted up front — otherwise the guard's success is misleading and the failure surfaces later, at the changelog view, where its cause is far less obvious. Deciding to leave it unfixed was reasonable given it was confirmed and minor, but recording it matters precisely because a passing-but-wrong guard is invisible until it bites.\n\nThe three carried-forward items each describe accumulated, unverified state rather than an active failure. Forty-five skills append to a shared `execution.jsonl` that has never been audited — an append-only log with 45 writers and no reader review is a plausible source of both silent format drift and unbounded growth. Five `LIFEOS/TOOLS` entries are missing and predate the current work, meaning their absence was not caused by this cleanup and may have been silently in place for some time; the required decision is restore-or-remove, not merely to note the gap. Both are instances of the configuration drift catalogued in [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]], where nothing is broken loudly enough to force attention until an audit surfaces it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene]] — The earlier same-day session in the same repo; that session's `.claude/worktrees/` gitignore and explicit-path staging address the tracking side of worktree hygiene, this one the teardown side.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — Prior worktree failure modes, including `ExitWorktree remove` deleting a branch along with the directory; the `dcg` gate here prevents exactly that class of unintended ref loss.\n- [[connections/narrowest-sufficient-escalation-over-blanket-bypass]] — The gated branch delete was routed to the user rather than bypassed, matching the escalation discipline documented there.\n- [[concepts/browser-automation-stack-audit-mac]] — The `[ -w ]`/`[ -r ]` mismatch is the same class of defect as the stderr-swallowing precondition check found in `Interceptor`'s `SKILL.md` earlier the same day.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The unaudited `execution.jsonl` writers and the five missing `LIFEOS/TOOLS` entries are configuration drift of the kind that only surfaces during a deliberate audit pass.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The hook-rule block is a permission-layer refusal, and diagnosing it correctly requires distinguishing a rule gate from a missing prompt.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-15.md]] — Skills repo cleanup close-out (20:32): Codex stop-gate review loop `task-msupzx5t-beim0g` cancelled, all 8 merged worktrees removed leaving only `main`, work through `8b31d4f` merged and pushed with clean tree, `dcg` rule `core.git:branch-force-delete` blocking agent branch deletion, the eight stale `worktree-*` branch names, the `Telos/Workflows/Update.md` `[ -w ]`-vs-`[ -r ]` guard gap at line 127, three non-atomic Telos Update steps left as-is, 45 unaudited `execution.jsonl` writers, and five missing `LIFEOS/TOOLS` entries.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-permission-modes.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Permission Modes and Self-Granting Prohibition\"\naliases: [manual mode, auto mode, bypassPermissions, dangerously-skip-permissions, dontAsk defaultMode, permission allow rule, inline bang command, classifier blocks permission edits, self-granting blocked]\ntags: [claude-code, permissions, security, configuration, workflow, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-14\n---\n\n# Claude Code Permission Modes and Self-Granting Prohibition\n\nClaude Code exposes several distinct permission postures — manual, auto, `dontAsk`, and `bypassPermissions` — that differ in whether sensitive actions prompt the user, are silently adjudicated by the auto-mode classifier, or are permitted unconditionally. A 2026-08-11 session clarified these distinctions while working around a blocked `diskutil rename` call, and established that Claude cannot grant itself new permissions: the classifier blocks edits to its own permission configuration by design.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Claude cannot edit its own permission config.** Self-granting is blocked by design; a permission change requires either a user hand-edit of `settings.json` or the `/permissions` command. This is the same trust boundary previously observed blocking Startup-folder writes during the token-dashboard install.\n- **Manual mode ≠ bypass mode — they are opposites.** Manual mode prompts on every sensitive action; auto mode lets the classifier decide silently (and silently *block*); `bypassPermissions` (`--dangerously-skip-permissions`) removes all guardrails. A user asking to \"enable manual mode\" to get more freedom has the polarity backwards.\n- **Auto mode can silently block what manual mode would have approved.** The `diskutil rename` was refused under auto mode but succeeded under manual mode via an ordinary approval prompt — no bypass was required.\n- **Three escalation paths, narrowest first:** (1) an inline `!` one-off (`! diskutil rename ...`), (2) a scoped allow rule such as `Bash(diskutil rename:*)`, (3) `\"defaultMode\": \"dontAsk\"` as a middle ground. Bypass mode is the last resort.\n- **Prefer the narrow fix on unattended machines.** Bypass was declined because the machine runs unattended background jobs (backups, nightly reindex, compiler drains); a targeted allow rule or `!` one-off delivers roughly the same convenience without removing guardrails from processes running without a human present.\n\n## Details\n\nThe four postures form a spectrum of who adjudicates a sensitive action. In manual mode the user adjudicates every one, which is verbose but maximally transparent — and, counterintuitively, the *most permissive* in practice, because a human can approve anything the classifier would have refused. In auto mode the classifier adjudicates silently; this reduces prompt volume but introduces a failure mode where a legitimate operation is refused with no approval path surfaced, which is exactly what happened with the volume rename. `\"defaultMode\": \"dontAsk\"` sits between these, suppressing prompts without wholesale disabling of the permission system. `bypassPermissions`, invoked via `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, removes the guardrails entirely.\n\nA practical diagnostic follows from the auto-mode failure mode: when an action is refused, the first question is not \"which permission do I need to add?\" but \"would a more transparent mode simply surface an approval path?\" Because auto mode's refusals are silent, they are easy to misread as policy prohibitions rather than as adjudication outcomes that a human could have overridden at a prompt. The 2026-08-11 case is the canonical example — the same `diskutil rename` that auto mode refused went through as a routine prompt under manual mode, meaning no configuration change was ever required.\n\nThe self-granting prohibition is the architecturally important part. When a permission gate blocks an action, the obvious remedy — have Claude add an allow rule for itself — is unavailable, because the classifier treats edits to permission configuration as a privileged operation independent of whatever the underlying action was. This mirrors the two-gate behavior documented during the token-dashboard install, where confirming execution of freshly-cloned code did not also authorize a Startup-folder write; permission escalation and the escalated action are separately gated. It is also consistent with the auto-mode classifier's refusal to edit files under `.claude/` observed during the 2026-06-25 health-check pass. The practical consequence is that permission friction must be resolved by the user out-of-band (`/permissions` or a hand-edit), or sidestepped by choosing a mode or one-off invocation that already covers the action. The 2026-08-11 resolution took the narrowest available path: switch to manual mode, approve the single rename at the prompt, and grant no standing permission at all, since the operation was one-off and had no expected recurrence.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-token-dashboard]] — Documents the auto-mode classifier's two independent trust gates (external-code execution, Startup-folder write); the self-granting prohibition here is the same class of boundary applied to permission config itself.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — Records that auto mode blocks edits under `.claude`; this article generalizes that observation into the full mode taxonomy and the escalation ladder.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The broader Claude Code configuration surface (`settings.json`, `/doctor`, plugin and MCP state) in which permission rules live.\n- [[concepts/fireflies-batch-rename-workflow]] — Another case where the auto-mode classifier false-positived and blocked legitimate work (Haiku subagent spawns), forcing a fallback path rather than surfacing an approval prompt.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The volume rename that triggered this clarification; resolved by a one-off manual-mode approval.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — End-of-day restore-closeout session (18:04): self-granting prohibition explained, manual/auto/`dontAsk`/bypass distinctions clarified, `diskutil rename` approved via manual-mode prompt, bypass declined in favor of narrower fixes on an unattended machine.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Skills Repo Git Hygiene: Worktree-Routed Edits and Explicit-Path Staging\"\naliases: [.claude/worktrees gitignore, nested repo hazard, background-isolation guard, explicit-path staging, skills repo commits, worktree-codex-direct-routing, anthropic-skills portability, mattpocock-skills Codex exclusions]\ntags: [git, claude-code, skills, workflow, tooling, worktree]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-15\nupdated: 2026-08-15\n---\n\n# Skills Repo Git Hygiene: Worktree-Routed Edits and Explicit-Path Staging\n\nThe 2026-08-15 wrap-up of a multi-session effort on the `~/.claude/skills` git repo established several durable practices for working in a repository that is simultaneously a live configuration directory and a version-controlled artifact. Edits are routed through git worktrees because a background-isolation guard blocks direct edits to the shared checkout; the `.claude/` directory is excluded from commits by explicit-path staging and then permanently by `.gitignore`, because `.claude/worktrees/` holds four live git worktrees that would nest repositories if committed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`.claude/worktrees/` must be gitignored.** It held four live git worktrees at the time of the session; committing it would nest repositories inside the repository. The exclusion was first achieved by explicit-path staging (deliberately naming every file to stage rather than using `git add -A`), then made permanent with a `.gitignore` line in commit `163db25`.\n- **The background-isolation guard blocks direct edits to the shared checkout**, so skill edits route through a git worktree off `main` rather than being applied in place.\n- **Commits shipped:** `faf6be8` (routing + description trims), `2c5a10a` (skill-authoring consolidation), `008f345` (doctor-plus, playwright-cli, presets gallery, 14 symlinks), `163db25` (`.gitignore`). `main` ended in sync with origin, working tree clean.\n- **Pre-existing uncommitted work was committed only after diff review plus a credential scan** (private repo, clean) — four to five files that had been sitting in the working tree across sessions.\n- **Two self-caught mistakes worth recording:** recommending removal of `anthropic-skills` (which would have destroyed portability), and a trim pass that silently dropped 20 trigger phrases while claiming it had not. The second was caught by the Codex review gate rather than by self-report.\n- **Open lever on Codex exclusions:** dropping `mattpocock-skills` is the only remaining option for reducing Codex's 171 excluded skills (down from 187) — undecided.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `~/.claude/skills` repository occupies an unusual position: it is the live configuration that the running agent reads from, and it is a git repository synced daily across machines. This dual role generates the hygiene requirements. Because it is live configuration, a background-isolation guard prevents direct writes to the shared checkout — an agent editing skills in place could alter the behavior of the very session performing the edit. Routing through a worktree off `main` isolates the edit until it is deliberately merged. Because it is a synced repository, deletions have cross-machine blast radius, which is why the [[concepts/browser-automation-stack-audit-mac]] remediation chose to neuter rather than delete two non-functional skills: the Windows machine may still have their dependencies.\n\nThe `.claude/worktrees/` problem is a specific instance of a general git hazard. Worktrees contain their own git metadata; committing a directory that holds four of them would embed repository state inside the tracked tree, producing the nested-repo confusion that git handles poorly and that is difficult to unwind after the fact. The two-step remedy is instructive: explicit-path staging solved it for the immediate commits (naming every intended file rather than relying on `git add -A` or `git add .`), and the `.gitignore` line made it structural so future sessions cannot reintroduce it by accident. Explicit-path staging generalizes as a discipline for any repository where the working tree contains a mixture of tracked source and live runtime state — the same class of caution as the [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] snapshotting, applied at the staging boundary rather than the destruction boundary. Committing the four-to-five pre-existing uncommitted files only after a diff review and a credential scan reflects the same posture: a private repo is not a reason to skip the scan, only a reason for the scan to be expected to come back clean.\n\nThe two self-caught mistakes are worth preserving because they illustrate different detection paths. The `anthropic-skills` removal recommendation was caught by reasoning — removing it would have destroyed the portability that the whole Codex/Claude Code effort was built to establish, so the consequence was visible before execution. The dropped trigger phrases were caught only by an external review gate, because the trimming pass's own account of its work was wrong: it reported preserving phrases it had in fact deleted. This is the same shape as the [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] pattern — an intermediate layer's self-report is not evidence — and it is the reason the outstanding batch trim of 36 over-ceiling skills described in [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] needs diff-level verification rather than a completion claim.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The substantive work these commits carried; the outstanding 36-skill batch trim will need the same worktree routing and diff verification.\n- [[concepts/browser-automation-stack-audit-mac]] — The daily cross-machine sync of this repo is precisely why dead skills were to be neutered rather than deleted.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — Prior worktree failure modes in the same environment; the `.claude/worktrees/` gitignore addition parallels the earlier one made for the same reason.\n- [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] — Explicit-path staging is the staging-boundary analogue of snapshotting before destructive git operations.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The trim pass's false self-report about trigger phrases, caught by an independent review gate.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — A prior incident in the same skills-repo lineage where what was committed (mode-120000 symlinks) caused cross-platform corruption; `008f345` here committed 14 symlinks.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-15.md]] — Skills repo wrap-up session (13:42): worktree-routed edits due to the background-isolation guard, `.claude/worktrees/` four live worktrees and nested-repo hazard, explicit-path staging then `.gitignore` (`163db25`), commits `faf6be8` / `2c5a10a` / `008f345` / `163db25`, diff review plus credential scan before committing pre-existing work, two self-caught mistakes (`anthropic-skills` removal, 20 dropped trigger phrases), `mattpocock-skills` as the remaining lever on Codex's 171 excluded skills.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/at-utilization-benchmarks-project.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"AT Utilization Benchmarks Project (Baylor Athletic Training / Kitman)\"\naliases: [AT_Utilization_Benchmarks, Kitman utilization, AT utilization metrics, Metric Dictionary, build_metric_dictionary_docx, vendor-mediated data access, Kitman API locked, one-shot request spec, AT utilization reporting, python-docx AT deliverable, Carrie alignment, utilization brief, 17 Baylor sports, complete coverage brief]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, athletic-training, kitman, data-pipeline, project-management, python, deliverables, vendor-mediation]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-09\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# AT Utilization Benchmarks Project (Baylor Athletic Training / Kitman)\n\nOn 2026-06-09, the AT_Utilization_Benchmarks project was built out following two back-to-back meetings — \"EOY Kitman Utilization\" (Jun 8) and \"Athletic Training — Utilization Metrics Standardization\" (Jun 9). The session produced a humanized Metric Dictionary v2 with a rebuildable Word deliverable via `python-docx`, and surfaced a critical constraint: Kitman API access is fully vendor-mediated, requiring a fundamental shift in data strategy from iterative incremental pulls to a single comprehensive, precisely-specified request. On 2026-06-18, a cleanup pass on the utilization brief resolved an outstanding \"missing 18th sport\" question — Baylor sponsors 17 varsity sports, so the brief already covered 100% of programs — and corrected the brief's stored file path.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Metric Dictionary v1 → v2 (humanizer pass):** removed em-dashes, bold-label scaffolding, and template-rhythm artifacts from v1; v2 reads as a practitioner document rather than a template output. A Word version was built via `python-docx` (`scripts/build_metric_dictionary_docx.py`) with Baylor green headings — pandoc is unavailable in this environment, making the direct Python approach the only scriptable option. The script is rebuildable from source on demand.\n- **Kitman API lockout — vendor-mediated only:** no direct API access exists at this contract tier. All data pulls flow through the Kitman vendor, killing iterative request cycles. Strategic implication: design one comprehensive, precise data request rather than requesting incrementally. Every field omitted costs a full vendor-mediation round trip. The access constraint also doubles as contract-renegotiation evidence: 17 months remain on the Kitman contract, and paying license fees while needing vendor mediation for access to one's own data is a concrete negotiating point.\n- **One-shot request spec design:** target: all teams, CSV format (not PDF), covering treatment, injury, availability, and roster data. The spec must enumerate every field and filter needed across all downstream use cases before submission — second requests for missed fields represent full round trips in calendar time.\n- **Carrie as gatekeeper and alignment partner:** emailing Carrie the Metric Dictionary docx both advances the project (her response gates downstream work) and prompts her to share her own documentation materials. Strategy for incoming docs: alignment pass, not a heavier rulebook — adopt her language where it overlaps the five core metrics, keep genuinely new asks minimal to reduce adoption friction.\n- **AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md pointer shim as default pattern:** the project was configured with `AGENTS.md` as the harness-agnostic source of truth and `CLAUDE.md` as a one-line pointer shim. This was explicitly saved to persistent memory as the user's preferred default for all future projects.\n- **Baylor sponsors 17 varsity sports (2026-06-18):** a \"missing 18th sport\" open item on the utilization brief was resolved by confirming the roster — Baylor does not offer beach volleyball, so the correct count is 17, and the brief already covered 100% of Baylor's programs (17/17). The brief's stored file path was corrected from `D:\\Dev\\Programs\\...` to `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\...`, and the open \"which team is uncovered?\" question was removed as resolved.\n- **Sensitivity rules:** trainer names are anonymized in all outbound documents; athlete medical data in `data/` stays local and is never included in shareable outputs or committed to remote repositories.\n\n## Details\n\nThe Kitman API constraint is the most architecturally consequential finding from this project. Unlike the Catapult and Perch integrations — where an API exists but behaves unexpectedly from first assumptions (see [[connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction]]) — Kitman offers no API access at all for this contract tier. This eliminates the typical iterative data-exploration workflow (request, inspect, identify gaps, re-request) and replaces it with a single-shot specification design problem. The analyst must enumerate every field, filter, team scope, and format needed across all downstream use cases before submitting the request, because each follow-up is a vendor-mediated round trip measured in calendar days rather than API response times. The framing of Kitman's vendor mediation as contract-renegotiation evidence is strategically useful — making the case for API access in the next contract cycle is strengthened when the gap between paying fees and needing vendor help to access one's own data is documented concretely.\n\nThe `python-docx` deliverable pattern establishes a reusable technique for environments where the standard document-conversion pipeline (pandoc) is unavailable. The `scripts/build_metric_dictionary_docx.py` script takes structured metric definitions and produces a formatted `.docx` with organizational branding, making the deliverable reproducible from source. Word was chosen over PDF as the primary deliverable format because AT staff and clinical collaborators are more likely to annotate, forward, and edit a `.docx` than a PDF.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 cleanup pass illustrates a small but generalizable data-hygiene lesson: when a count discrepancy appears in a deliverable (\"the table shows 17 sports but Baylor has 18 — which one is missing?\"), the correct first move is to verify the source roster rather than assume the table has a gap. Here the apparent gap did not exist — Baylor sponsors exactly 17 varsity sports and does not field beach volleyball, so the brief was already complete at 17/17. Confirming the roster both closed the open question and prevented a spurious \"add the missing sport\" edit that would have introduced an error. The same pass corrected the brief's stored path (the memory entry pointed to a stale `D:\\Dev\\Programs\\...` location rather than the actual `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\...` file), synchronizing the daily log and memory record with the on-disk reality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]] — The AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md pointer-shim adopted for this project is the import-approach variant of the dual-context-file pattern.\n- [[connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction]] — The Kitman vendor-mediation constraint is one pattern in Baylor's sports-tech integration landscape alongside Catapult's token complexity, Perch's auth-scheme surprises, and Kinexon's CSM-gated docs; all share the property that naive integration assumptions fail.\n- [[concepts/bu-cheer-summer-2026-training]] — Another Baylor Athletics deliverable where `python-docx`/`openpyxl` were chosen because standard conversion tools were unavailable; both produce rebuildable, script-driven document artifacts.\n- [[concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring]] — A concurrent Baylor project also advancing athlete monitoring capability; the two represent complementary layers of an emerging applied performance analytics stack.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — AT utilization session (17:13): metric dictionary humanizer pass, python-docx deliverable and rebuild script, Kitman API lockout discovery, one-shot request spec strategy, Carrie alignment pass approach, AGENTS.md default preference saved to persistent memory, sensitivity rules codified.\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Utilization-brief cleanup (09:37, 10:43): resolved the \"missing 18th sport\" open item (Baylor sponsors 17 varsity sports; no beach volleyball; brief already 17/17 complete), corrected the brief's stored file path to `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\...`, removed the resolved open question.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/athlete-monitoring-rtp-presentation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Athlete Monitoring / Return-to-Play Conference Presentation Design\"\naliases: [RTP presentation, athlete monitoring slide deck, sports science conference slides, z-score traffic light thresholds, stakeholder report slide, frontend-slides sports science, visual-first sports presentation, 11-slide RTP structure, 14-slide RTP deck, single-athlete framing presentation, on-dark CSS slide deck, synthetic keydown browser automation unreliable, You Have the Data Now What, RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT, RTP deck v1 v2, slide 8 chart overflow fix, twenty-minute gap analysis]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, sports-science, presentation, communication, return-to-play, athlete-monitoring, workflow, deliverables]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-17.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-17\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Athlete Monitoring / Return-to-Play Conference Presentation Design\n\nOn 2026-06-17, a conference presentation slide deck (~20-min talk) was designed for a visually-oriented sports science audience covering athlete monitoring and return-to-play data workflows. The design process resolved four key architectural decisions — framing, statistical thresholds, content constraints, and audience philosophy — and produced a locked 11-slide running order built around a single-athlete RTP narrative with minimal text and strong visual anchors. A subsequent validation pass confirmed all slides were layout-clean and fixed a dark-mode visibility bug on the final slide. On 2026-06-18, a full speaker script was written for the deck (`RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT.md`), a duration analysis showed the 11-slide deck ran far short of its 20-minute slot, and the deck was expanded to 14 slides (v2) with v1 preserved.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Single-athlete framing throughout:** roster-comparison views were explicitly rejected as \"off-topic for the individual RTP framing.\" The presentation's decision-support narrative only holds together when the audience follows one athlete's trajectory from injury through return-to-competition decisions. Adding a roster view introduces a different clinical use case that fragments narrative coherence.\n- **Visual-first audience philosophy:** the intended audience \"eats with their eyes\" — conversational delivery carries the explanatory load while slides provide visual anchors, not text. Minimal text per slide; strong imagery and color-coded indicators are the design primitives.\n- **Z-score traffic-light thresholds: −1.5 SD / −2 SD.** Applied in the Excel demo to translate raw metrics into actionable status flags. −1.5 SD = caution/yellow; −2 SD = concern/red. The leave-behind template implements the same logic, ensuring the presentation and the artifact practitioners actually use share the same statistical foundation.\n- **Screenshots-only (no live build):** consistent with the leave-behind template format. All data visualization examples appear as screenshots rather than live-built charts, eliminating technical failure risk in a conference setting.\n- **11-slide running order:** bridge → decision-first framing (×2) → generalizing → metric selection → blank template → populated/flagged → charted → stakeholder report → take-home materials → close.\n- **Slide 9 — Sample Stakeholder Report:** single-athlete trend chart + color-coded status flag + one plain-language interpretive line. Models the exact artifact the audience would produce and deliver to coaching/medical staff, completing the \"here is the workflow\" → \"here is the output\" arc.\n- **Josse referenced verbally only (not on-screen):** a named methodological reference was kept off slides for sensitivity reasons; verbal attribution suffices for academic credentialing without creating a circulating visual artifact.\n- **Slide validation (Session 23:56):** All 11 slides confirmed layout-clean at 1440×900 and 375×667. Synthetic keyboard event dispatch (`keydown`) is unreliable for slide navigation in browser automation — smooth-scroll does not complete on synthetic key events in browser extension automation contexts. Use direct navigation-dot element clicks for reliable automated HTML slide deck validation.\n- **Dark-background slides require adaptive nav chrome:** Slide 11 uses a dark background (`rgb(17,17,17)`); the default nav-dot and slide-counter colors (`rgba(0,0,0,0.45)`) are invisible against it. Fix: an `on-dark` CSS class toggled on `<body>` by the `setActive()` JavaScript function enables CSS overrides for nav elements on dark slides. This pattern generalizes to any HTML slide deck with alternating light/dark slide backgrounds.\n- **A tight, purposeful deck runs far shorter than its slot (2026-06-18):** the 11-slide \"You Have the Data. Now What?\" deck at correct density runs ~5–7 minutes (7–9 with pauses), leaving an 11–14 minute gap against the 20-minute target. Closing the gap requires real content (case-study athlete walkthrough, live-demo narration for the data table and chart slides, planted audience Q&A), not padding — slide count and density are the drivers, not verbosity.\n- **Speaker script + v1/v2 versioning (2026-06-18):** a full per-slide speaker script with talking points, notes, and pacing was saved to `outputs/presentation/RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT.md`. The deck was expanded to 14 slides; the original 11-slide version was reconstructed from git and preserved as `RTP-Data-Collection-v1.html`, the 14-slide version saved as `RTP-Data-Collection-v2.html`, with the working file `RTP-Data-Collection.html` aliasing v2. Preserving v1 before editing follows an \"always keep the original\" versioning discipline.\n- **Slide 8 chart overflow fix (2026-06-18):** the slide-8 line chart overflowed at short/landscape viewports (~896×414); fixed with `aspect-ratio` plus `max-height: min(40vh, 320px)`. This is a second responsive-layout defect (alongside the slide-11 dark-theme fix) that only surfaced at non-standard viewport sizes, reinforcing multi-viewport render verification.\n\n## Details\n\nThe presentation's core design challenge is bridging the gap between analytical rigor and practitioner legibility for a mixed audience of sports scientists, athletic trainers, and coaches. A talk that leads with methodology (sensor setup, reliability coefficients, statistical assumptions) loses practitioners in the first two minutes; a talk that shows only colored flags without any basis loses the sports scientists. The \"decision-first framing (×2)\" in the running order addresses this: the talk opens with the end product — the practitioner's decision — and only then traces backward to the analytical workflow that supports it. The repetition reinforces the framing before technical detail begins.\n\nThe z-score threshold design (−1.5 / −2 SD) was chosen for the Excel demo because it is transferable: any clinician working with any monitoring dataset can understand and apply standard deviation cutoffs without statistical training beyond \"how far from the average.\" The traffic-light metaphor (green/yellow/red) maps intuitively to go/caution/stop decisions without requiring the audience to interpret numbers directly. The leave-behind template implements the same −1.5 / −2 SD thresholds automatically, so practitioners who use it after the session are working with the same statistical logic demonstrated in the talk — reducing drift toward arbitrary threshold modification post-session.\n\nSlide 9's stakeholder report design was added specifically to address the \"so what do I hand to the coach/AT?\" question that sports scientists routinely face after building a monitoring pipeline. The three-element structure (trend chart + status flag + one interpretive sentence) is the minimum viable stakeholder report: more complex outputs tend to go unread or generate confusion when shared with coaching staff who do not routinely interpret data visualizations. The time budget for the talk was kept deliberately soft — enriching content depth was prioritized over strict adherence to 20 minutes, on the premise that a substantive overrun is less damaging than a rushed, thin presentation for an engaged sports science audience. The frontend-slides skill was loaded after all content decisions were locked to execute the build phase.\n\nFollowing the content design session (20:28), a validation pass (Session 23:56) confirmed all 11 slides rendered correctly at both standard and mobile viewport sizes. The initial browser automation approach used synthetic keydown events to navigate between slides — this proved unreliable because the presentation's smooth-scroll transition does not complete when triggered by a synthetic key event in browser extension automation contexts. Switching to direct navigation-dot element clicks resolved this. A dark-mode visibility bug was discovered on Slide 11, whose dark background (`rgb(17,17,17)`) made both the slide counter text and the inactive navigation dots invisible in their default dark-gray color (`rgba(0,0,0,0.45)`). The fix uses a `setActive()` JavaScript function that toggles an `on-dark` class on the `<body>` element each time a new slide becomes active.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 work moved the deck from a validated visual artifact toward a delivery-ready talk. Writing the speaker script exposed a duration mismatch that the slide count alone had masked: a deck deliberately built for high visual density and low text runs quickly when narrated, so the 11-slide deck consumed only ~5–7 minutes of a 20-minute slot. The assistant explicitly declined to \"pad\" the existing script and instead framed three substance-based gap-closure paths (expand the script in place, add 4–6 slides, or a hybrid), identifying the data-table and chart slides, a case-study athlete walkthrough, and planted audience-participation moments as the highest-value additions. The deck was then expanded to 14 slides (v2), and the original 11-slide deck was reconstructed from git and preserved as v1 — an instance of keeping the prior version before making a structural edit. A second responsive defect was fixed during this pass: the slide-8 line chart overflowed at short landscape viewports and was constrained with `aspect-ratio` and a `max-height: min(40vh, 320px)` cap, echoing the slide-11 dark-theme fix as a layout bug invisible at the default viewport but present at edge sizes.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] — Individual RTC reports built from VALD ForceDecks data for a single athlete; shares the single-athlete framing, Baylor institutional branding, and color-coded status indicators (Exceeds Baseline / Progressing) with the stakeholder report slide design.\n- [[concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring]] — SPC-based monitoring dashboard with first-class explainability requirements for coaching and medical staff; shares the \"every metric needs a plain-language justification\" communication principle and threshold-based flag logic.\n- [[concepts/baylor-wtn-acwr-analysis]] — The dual-model (actual vs. compliant counterfactual) ACWR overlay is another athlete monitoring communication artifact designed to make statistical content interpretable for practitioners — the same decision-support framing used in the presentation.\n- [[connections/athlete-monitoring-communication-grammar]] — The cross-cutting visual vocabulary (color coding, threshold flags, trend + flag + sentence) that independently recurs across the presentation, Zinnecker reports, Tezino SPC monitoring, and the Swiss Modern coaching deck.\n- [[concepts/sports-coaching-slide-design-system]] — A parallel S&C coaching staff slide deck built the same day (Session 23:43) using the Swiss Modern preset; shares the traffic-light color constraint and HTML slide build context. The two projects together document complementary implementation gotchas: coaching deck (box-sizing, file:// URL mangling) and RTP presentation (synthetic keydown unreliability, dark-background CSS toggle, slide-8 chart overflow).\n- [[concepts/rtp-clinic-deliverable-finalization]] — The parallel 31-slide Eric+Jeremy clinic deck; both efforts involve speaker-script restructuring, versioned deck files, and multi-viewport render verification, but this is the shorter single-author conference talk.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-17.md]] — Conference presentation design session (20:28): four design decisions locked, 11-slide running order established, Slide 9 stakeholder report format designed. Validation session (23:56): all 11 slides confirmed layout-clean at 1440×900 and 375×667, synthetic keydown unreliable, switched to dot-click navigation, dark-background Slide 11 visibility bug fixed via `on-dark` body class toggle.\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Speaker-script and duration session (01:12): full per-slide script saved to `RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT.md`, 11-slide deck measured at ~5–7 min vs 20-min slot, three substance-based gap-closure options framed. Versioning/bug-fix session (01:29): deck expanded to 14 slides (v2), original preserved as v1, slide-8 chart overflow fixed with `aspect-ratio` + `max-height: min(40vh, 320px)`.\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 266) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-12.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-12\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (17:31)\n\n**Context:** Continuing the multi-task \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) — making the skills/backup stack work on both Mac and Windows. Tasks 1–2 complete; paused, then resumed at round 10 of plan review.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Codex hit its usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM). User said \"use claude, that fine\" → reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session (resume can't cross providers).\n- The Claude reviewer found what Codex missed: **14 of 19** outward symlinks in the skills repo are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). Task 4 would have copied all of them into Eric's personal repo.\n- Reframed the inventory: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links.**\n- Round-10 review findings (plan not yet approved): (1) serious — the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \"committed\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor — text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor — the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\n- User said \"pause\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \"resume\".\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo — same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely — leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** — the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** — the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n\n### Session (23:30)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count — SSD capture included).\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in — it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way — Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-13T00:08:07-05:00] compile | 2026-09-12.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-12.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-12.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
